WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.640
In 2013, IBM released the smallest movie ever made.

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:08.720
Its a simple stop-motion short film but what makes it special is that they controlled the placement of individual atoms

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.500
to tell their story.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.880
Everyone knows that atoms are tiny. They're the building blocks of all matter.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.400
But I don't think people fully appreciate how small atoms really are.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.920
You see, you cant just use a regular microscope to look at atoms.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.080
They're literally too small to interact with light, making them invisible.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.200
You have to use a special machine called a Scanning Tunneling Microscope

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:32.640
Which basically maps the electrical charges of atoms to create a model representation.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.200
Like what the researchers at IBM did to make their movie.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.200
Even though it seems like we are looking at individual atoms,

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.180
we're really not.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:41.160
So what does an atom actually look like?

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:45.580
I mean if we were gonna scale one up to the size of a tennis ball it would probably look like this: nothing!

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.420
And that's because atoms are mostly empty space.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.700
Let's take a simple atom like hydrogen, for example.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:56.680
It consists of just  an electron and a proton but the electron doesn't simply orbit around the proton

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:58.220
the same way a planet orbits around a star

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:01.260
Instead, there's a field which shows where the electron might be found.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.700
And these fields are what define the size of an atom.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:07.080
The shape of these fields can get kind of weird when you start adding more electrons,

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.440
but for hydrogen it's simply a sphere.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.960
To really drive home just how empty atoms really are,

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:15.100
if we were to instead scale a proton up to this size,

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.860
the entire atom would be 8 kilometers wide!

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.980
That means this hydrogen atom is the size of downtown Los Angeles,

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.100
with the tiny electron somewhere within this sphere.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.260
It might be 4 kilometers that way, or 4 kilometers this way,

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:30.640
up there, down here,

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:31.880
or anywhere in between.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:34.580
If you thought finding a needle in a haystack was impossible,

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.600
try finding an electron in an atom.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:41.320
But let's go back to when our atom was the size of a tennis ball. And, atoms come in various sizes,

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:47.020
but for this video I'm making the assumption that an atom is one ångström wide, or a tenth of a nanometer.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:50.780
Our tennis ball is 67 millimeters wide, making our scale

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.000
670 million to 1.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.680
A group of atoms like this is called a molecule, and this one in particular's name

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:00.480
is adenine. And, there's nothing special about these individually,

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.980
they're fairly simple. But when joined together,

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:08.220
they become something very cool. They become the source code of all life: DNA.

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:10.160
Now, traditional models of DNA

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.380
are fairly simple in form. But the problem is by showing them as a literal twisted ladder,

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.220
you don't have the context for how small DNA really is.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.520
Because in reality, it's like 20 atoms wide.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.240
It's very, very tiny.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.500
And this chain of molecules would stack millions of times

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.200
to form a single strand of DNA stretching thousands of kilometers in length,

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.280
way out into space at this scale.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:34.000
Molecules get progressively more complex as the groupings become larger,

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.300
such as with proteins, organelles, and viruses.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:42.480
But at a certain point they become so complex that they become full on molecule economies,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.720
and we call those cells.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:46.620
Let's take a look at blood!

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.280
A red blood cell is about 7 microns (0.007 mm) across,

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.840
making it one of the smallest cells in the human body.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.040
It's important because it acts like a FedEx truck delivering oxygen to the whole body.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:59.020
But even though our scaled DNA would be about a meter wide,

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:02.100
a red blood cell would be 5 kilometers wide.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:06.380
They would appear in the sky like alien dropships coming to exterminate humanity.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.040
A single drop of blood contains 5 million red blood cells.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.800
In fact, your body produces 2 million of these every second!

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.960
There are trillions of these running through your veins right now!

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.300
That is such a crazy fact that I wouldn't be surprised if you took that with a grain of salt, I mean...

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:38.480
what if we did!

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.480
Regular table salt is just a compound of sodium and chlorine,

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:45.800
and thanks to the way these atoms are packed together, they form these really cool looking cubic crystals.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:52.740
Because a typical table salt crystal is about half a millimeter wide, that scales up to over 300 kilometers!

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:56.620
This is several times larger than the asteroid that killed off all the dinosaurs.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.760
An impact of this size would reduce the entire planet to lava,

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.200
and this guy would be the destroyer of worlds.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.440
So it's obvious that with our atomic scale, even tiny things become massive.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.320
But when I was crunching the numbers for this video, I discovered something really weird about pennies!

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.900
I measured this penny at 19.02 millimeters wide.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.420
When you multiply that by our scale of 670 million,

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:23.240
you end up with a penny 12,743 kilometers wide.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:27.120
Now why is that special? Well, the Earth's diameter at its equator is:

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:35.780
Somehow, the penny at this atomic scale is the same size as our planet,

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:38.220
with a difference of only a single kilometer.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.060
If we put that into perspective, the thickness of the penny would appear:

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:47.180
And this really helps put into context just how small atoms really are.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:51.420
Just imagine a giant planet sized disk filled with tennis balls.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:57.160
But now let's have some fun. Remember when we scaled a proton up to a tennis ball and how big the whole atom got?

00:04:57.860 --> 00:04:59.860
How big would a penny be at that scale?

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:01.080
It would be:

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:07.420
That is the size of Jupiter's orbit around the Sun.

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:11.360
That is incredibly huge. But can we go even further?

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:14.580
Just like an atom, a proton is also mostly empty space.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.200
It's actually composed of a bunch of subatomic particles that we call quarks.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:23.600
According to the standard model, quarks are actual literal points with no physical size at all.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.400
Cause size is kinda irrelevant at the quantum level.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.920
However, there is some evidence that perhaps the quark is about 2000 times smaller than a proton.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:35.640
So, if you were to scale a quark up to the size of a tennis ball,

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:37.500
how big would our penny be then?

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:43.080
It would become hundreds of times bigger than our entire solar system, coming at just under:

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:49.520
But, now we're on the cosmic scale and suddenly this massive penny becomes tiny again.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:53.940
I mean our closest neighbouring star Alpha Centauri is about 4 lightyears away.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:57.300
It would take 14 of these pennies just to reach it.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:01.340
In comparison, our Milky Way galaxy is 100,000 lightyears across.

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:03.940
What do we have to do to put a dent into that scale?

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:10.300
There's a unit of distance that is considered to be the smallest length mathematically possible before the laws of physics fall apart.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:11.940
It is called the Planck length.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:15.820
[Plank Guy] Now this is the length of a plank but this is not what a Planck length is.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.120
[Wren] It measures out to:

